WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.572
A useful definition of agentic A I is a bounded decision loop.

00:00:04.572 --> 00:00:07.752
The system begins with a goal or request.

00:00:07.752 --> 00:00:15.502
It keeps a visible working state, such as the user question, evidence already collected, the current step number,

00:00:15.502 --> 00:00:20.632
and any important constraints. It then chooses an approved action.

00:00:20.632 --> 00:00:28.549
That action might be retrieval, a database lookup, a calculation, a web search, or generation.

00:00:28.999 --> 00:00:33.011
After the action runs, the result becomes an observation.

00:00:33.011 --> 00:00:36.636
The system updates state and decides again.

00:00:36.636 --> 00:00:40.711
This cycle can repeat, but it must be bounded.

00:00:40.711 --> 00:00:47.021
There should always be a success condition, a failure condition, or a human handoff.

00:00:47.021 --> 00:00:51.606
For example, imagine a support agent checking a refund.

00:00:51.606 --> 00:00:59.641
It may retrieve the refund policy, observe that the policy alone is not enough, look up the actual order date,

00:00:59.641 --> 00:01:02.898
compare that date with the policy, and then stop.

00:01:03.411 --> 00:01:05.468
Notice what makes this agentic.

00:01:05.468 --> 00:01:08.473
It is not simply using a powerful model.

00:01:08.473 --> 00:01:14.131
The important behavior is repeated state update and controlled choice of the next action.
